#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124223 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "922b0f79-2c87-56f9-bf8a-18711a2a09d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4838" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. What is $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/0dce1fb65e0b82525b6d461b3641b10eb6d3641f590d46af6fa4af9f2e020dc6.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C},$ we find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124224 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "a3911ca2-a357-5f37-8abc-e01e5518f24b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4840" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ to $\\mathcal{C} = \\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ is given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ given that $\\mathbf{x} =2\\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/acdc98dc5a658e7c945dca00c1a1c0a73d87e3dc98db8071a534a4fd7f215c90.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)(-4)-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/883431c76485c9fd706fe79c2bf3936946cfb0dba602ebd0df77d6cc9e800665.png)\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124225 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "a2d3629f-5b7c-5b65-998f-8754ff11e7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4841" :question/problem "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/0dcc93449ac1d14e51850e4a74f639aeb3acd21b720ba2b9deeb2208d9fffeb4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ As a result, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/50bbd2dd0fa1c0732a01d07c2614ab15549272fcc0b7d686651f0464b3b50f39.png)\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 10-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124302 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "f04f55d3-40ec-52a8-a762-d8ac7090b8b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7276" :question/problem "The bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/c6c10ed068cfde656d178c98eb4af979563efc82d589f750b8fa92973f362e2a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/034a2a4079120d719f9061c339899f9aed9b50a765200291ca15c500ef647a70.png)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/8d07812ea170bcbb56264468f4cfd0fa8b88cf4f68f9b50d35f61e9e33de45c3.png)\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dd962a2-63d7-5abf-89e3-d3ba1e837040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351761} {:answer/id #uuid "4bfd7222-a523-581a-a218-f3bb2936bca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351762} {:answer/id #uuid "0b666fa5-1538-5468-bbd2-e2309cd1f587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351763} {:answer/id #uuid "cefb5be7-b539-520f-8ec3-1387c88b26e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351764} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea4945d-1f66-5c86-a8fb-23f95aa7dc0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea4945d-1f66-5c86-a8fb-23f95aa7dc0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351765} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbcaf2d0-b378-5f2d-9c3e-bc528c5dc797" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "acf64a31-7456-52ad-869b-85c33c0fbc89" :question/math-academy-id "q-113556" :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4{b}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4{b}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3e6991e-f860-548b-be8b-297e46bda84f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351768} {:answer/id #uuid "f1931604-4acf-523d-aed9-13e8d96edb97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351769} {:answer/id #uuid "444c9c1f-dde7-5beb-bc9b-e74de8b0ba22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351770} {:answer/id #uuid "41ded1e6-b152-57ea-b153-bf54dd929790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351771} {:answer/id #uuid "ad94b668-99ab-5cf4-95c9-55509cb61969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41ded1e6-b152-57ea-b153-bf54dd929790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351771} :answer-field/id #uuid "caa87502-fd2a-56e5-9869-e8a9867fc34f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c0f7621-5804-5852-9801-58afb60153f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-113557" :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=5{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-{b}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=5{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce2d6e2-f798-5844-b19f-24a867a1d05c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351775} {:answer/id #uuid "6874fa14-0d02-5c30-8830-2a63d455a6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351776} {:answer/id #uuid "4411aeb9-ad19-5330-ac71-5cad4405e27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351777} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec6b23e-ea2f-5ecf-a209-4e6fb81c9c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351778} {:answer/id #uuid "28ebc061-25b9-59ba-94f6-c87435e3473c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ec6b23e-ea2f-5ecf-a209-4e6fb81c9c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351778} :answer-field/id #uuid "154d6588-74c6-5ab1-bbf5-df0cd9901e5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ed39867-0f3e-5f3d-ab65-536828c9ba67" :question/math-academy-id "q-113573" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-8)-(-3)\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06a9c4b2-ebfc-5072-9f30-9c6531214443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351782} {:answer/id #uuid "21cd7bd8-853e-5079-a2b2-721d3f3f62eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351783} {:answer/id #uuid "d17e0ee8-6ce9-562e-ab26-3e7a77e0bed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351784} {:answer/id #uuid "3df5c619-45ff-5560-8d34-b9c04c34658b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351785} {:answer/id #uuid "3d96d5cb-02b4-57d5-832d-87245c082ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3df5c619-45ff-5560-8d34-b9c04c34658b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351785} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9693b81-a126-53c8-b953-7e75595ff4e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23f9d4a3-d1ff-5ea8-befa-ca07b6f0d60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113600" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b20ed1-2ce6-5fb4-bac4-40be33a3c82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294464} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4b6445-8671-5d29-88ab-f549b8ddf893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294465} {:answer/id #uuid "4e7c956d-97f4-5416-9982-9145cb460462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294466} {:answer/id #uuid "52a27d75-4b7b-5e39-825c-1cf4c4c2d76c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294467} {:answer/id #uuid "9a438c04-4c8b-55b9-8a84-1823140209ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a438c04-4c8b-55b9-8a84-1823140209ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294468} :answer-field/id #uuid "c741a8e2-0bfc-5036-a313-875ab9cac0ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "898a9b69-1ce6-510f-b039-ca6d19ae6353" :question/math-academy-id "q-113606" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 6-(-7)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd1977de-45ab-5e22-b735-0ca3cccc02a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351789} {:answer/id #uuid "70d1b2a9-f8d1-5378-aebc-b18be0f2d28c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351790} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7e9078-7c45-534a-b620-1ee7051832eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351791} {:answer/id #uuid "67592d84-9886-5622-b298-c8037aa2b414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351792} {:answer/id #uuid "63b2da16-d106-5149-a66e-33a56a72527a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf7e9078-7c45-534a-b620-1ee7051832eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351791} :answer-field/id #uuid "afd79649-6f53-5262-8b5e-773cc171363f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0151fa01-e373-5136-9f5d-f3422f4a30ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-113689" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -11 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 13 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -11 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 7 \\\\ -11 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & 7 \\\\ -11 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a45e63c4-2d20-59c3-b65a-154d78617a6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351796} {:answer/id #uuid "64995c4d-9d1a-5ab3-8824-1e7fbeb4ca3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351797} {:answer/id #uuid "a914f6de-3e33-59a1-93ae-37d7f910a9a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351798} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1b663f-93a2-5d68-8301-e72f7acf83e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 175921