ned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ea9fcf-9a0b-5bf1-844f-7ad327a9b73c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351803} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad89b6c-af4f-51eb-bdb5-6764171fda27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351804} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2fee1f-9b07-5d5f-87ae-0449a1b22f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351805} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e02490-3a3e-516d-a4d7-ab737d23bfd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351806} {:answer/id #uuid "c621c76b-b20c-53e3-80db-7819a93cdc47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09ea9fcf-9a0b-5bf1-844f-7ad327a9b73c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351803} :answer-field/id #uuid "545fbe08-2b44-506d-b061-9b40048958ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "858d9af2-f7a3-5372-8435-4e3c1f733534" :question/math-academy-id "q-113708" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea153dcc-b309-539e-bc1b-3fef6ac3a406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351810} {:answer/id #uuid "c701765f-670f-5bef-b124-f54e4a45a156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351811} {:answer/id #uuid "dd29d5ba-3e0c-5eb9-abf2-349383038c06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351812} {:answer/id #uuid "25ccef5a-1140-55fe-aa99-9a26fe9a58ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351813} {:answer/id #uuid "f88da7d3-2646-51f4-8619-71d947056628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ccef5a-1140-55fe-aa99-9a26fe9a58ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351813} :answer-field/id #uuid "85f68bfd-8644-5d84-89c4-db0fa6252365" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78eaadfd-8ca1-5dc2-b684-8e302cf66a53" :question/math-academy-id "q-36485" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91464b5f-ff02-517e-b602-2b282f4031eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 22\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122483} {:answer/id #uuid "962dc444-fa59-50b8-a22a-00e27ecba948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122484} {:answer/id #uuid "964494b9-301b-50d4-b337-1923f7356fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122485} {:answer/id #uuid "0887e955-50ac-5551-9861-2f9d77c14401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122486} {:answer/id #uuid "2996e578-f4b9-55be-82a3-8f336b0b508c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2996e578-f4b9-55be-82a3-8f336b0b508c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122487} :answer-field/id #uuid "862933da-272f-5db5-90aa-7c6cc52fd38c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f503090a-50ea-5c09-941f-e82b557a90db" :question/math-academy-id "q-36503" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31510a4e-0abd-5828-b4c6-a904dbafba78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351817} {:answer/id #uuid "1daecfb6-b2fd-59e9-baa8-5bfd13d72701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351818} {:answer/id #uuid "ce524dc9-d9a6-57fa-a496-d7e3df373555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351819} {:answer/id #uuid "933ab283-5429-52c4-a22c-de6d7291bd45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351820} {:answer/id #uuid "6e353d77-837e-577f-ab99-795911dd21b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce524dc9-d9a6-57fa-a496-d7e3df373555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351819} :answer-field/id #uuid "69f1f149-34c1-5bd9-bf85-3271cce69a03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de14b349-7606-50d6-9aa6-6afeb955679c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37152" :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=4{b}_{3}-2{b}_{1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=4{b}_{3}-2{b}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d86d8e11-3457-5f1a-924d-43518a3d606e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351824} {:answer/id #uuid "af139974-32c3-5912-96da-c984d7bcff4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351825} {:answer/id #uuid "33ac8d36-b0dd-5f4e-b1ab-d9a588a78e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351826} {:answer/id #uuid "cfda4a44-027b-5ec4-bb90-479dde81feb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-17 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351827} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cce4f2-ab