"1b38ee4d-7e5c-5b05-b3f7-a9698dfc2612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351832} {:answer/id #uuid "2a72c860-b475-5696-881b-d0eebcd791c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351833} {:answer/id #uuid "b955ffb2-d295-5482-8f7a-75999e7c5595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351834} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7f03a1-be68-52a4-8ff5-b3bf0003e488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d7e3025-aa12-5a30-890f-ff82d43c3a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351831} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed7ca470-5057-53c7-9e21-43e1ca183d27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50e979ba-7b97-5986-8a48-8c9960bb8901" :question/math-academy-id "q-82599" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=3{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot (-5)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=3{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93797343-f39b-5253-a57c-259d8af8990a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351838} {:answer/id #uuid "f36ed0a2-0729-5cdc-8262-db354881bb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351839} {:answer/id #uuid "75af1432-bcb0-5e00-9e3a-abbc3bf0e07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351840} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5fbb3c-7ca9-58bb-a1b7-9a9e452ab373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351841} {:answer/id #uuid "97d19949-449d-5a4f-b324-4c44d4904e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f36ed0a2-0729-5cdc-8262-db354881bb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351839} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3e1d407-5828-550a-869c-a3bedef1b8ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c7d63da-59ab-5308-9b89-bf650fb9db9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-82606" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5722a19c-9c8a-5b3d-acb8-7d8aa432b1c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351845} {:answer/id #uuid "4b862f5b-4e5d-5dc9-b495-3748172dc716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351846} {:answer/id #uuid "d64c21b9-ed97-576b-90e8-1b69dcb5e9a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351847} {:answer/id #uuid "33d71ede-cada-5028-91b4-2bcd9dc57b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351848} {:answer/id #uuid "1d56caab-fee8-5bcb-a88f-a1fb3ee19e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33d71ede-cada-5028-91b4-2bcd9dc57b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351848} :answer-field/id #uuid "601d36a9-7427-50eb-bf75-b9775ab51a77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13aef9df-8682-5fe0-a906-e0817933e236" :question/math-academy-id "q-82609" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "751ff497-1e37-568b-8593-ef8a78879686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351852} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1271aa-6cb7-5c26-9a09-15e1dd445007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351853} {:answer/id #uuid "9a69b189-4dd2-57da-9312-bc93fe6e0c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351854} {:answer/id #uuid "02de9fcd-37f8-5e79-9fb5-d8dbb7ed1e6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351855} {:answer/id #uuid "f21a9a4d-c2ea-59b6-9128-2d6781b4e5f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae1271aa-6cb7-5c26-9a09-15e1dd445007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351853} :answer-field/id #uuid "562c096d-6772-58f0-9e1b-96f327190b02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f70f6b70-ff67-53f2-9df7-7fc4b4b4c251" :question/math-academy-id "q-82618" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 18 & -29 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3803ac44-4e83-5cad-928f-4fd6eb59917c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351859} {:answer/id #uuid "92a5cf8e-1628-5d7a-8553-f5761782679b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351860} {:answer/id #uuid "2e131e91-4251-5c5d-a4a6-6865c73ff0ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351861} {:answer/id #uuid "44d23d21-437f-519f-9434-a130311626cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351862} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3cc109-eb69-5b08-b982-ede0f8b2458b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e131e91-4251-5c5d-a4a6-6865c73ff0ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351861} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6a8ddfb-f8fb-540b-91fc-597c8860d569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57c0c3fb-cd0e-588f-875c-0ee340ecbc73" :question/math-academy-id "q-82702" :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=3{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=3{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d767114-2165-5418-815c-c5f6a31b28be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351866} {:answer/id #uuid "ece2bb12-a445-5699-a1b3-4dfcdda1f733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351867} {:answer/id #uuid "0b9f4e4d-835d-55bb-9340-bbef4fe1694a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351868} {:answer/id #uuid "256bafd9-8fa1-5fa4-b57b-08e9dd1f4a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351869} {:answer/id #uuid "d8dfcfcb-71e3-5654-be6c-354969d0dbcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8dfcfcb-71e3-5654-be6c-354969d0dbcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351870} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf12b751-c51c-5fc0-90db-f298ee710324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aabb7d44-6631-59cb-8c14-d07df8ca4ee7" :question/math-academy-id "q-82705" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=-3{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 6-5\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=-3{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eec5fb2a-19da-564a-8d6a-ccae7569cce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351873} {:answer/id #uuid "01766fdb-471a-5d95-97db-f827c967d37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351874} {:answer/id #uuid "15c57bb7-f026-53c6-847e-baf6a41324b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351875} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d71378-186c-536b-94c2-50483d91220e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351876} {:answer/id #uuid "aa892a16-484c-5d8e-ae0c-eff3da5f831e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01766fdb-471a-5d95-97db-f827c967d37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351874} :answer-field/id #uuid "99eac267-fe36-52c0-99c7-2da0925288e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6bfae3e3-2776-5559-98d8-7fc88a34d766" :question/math-academy-id "q-82710" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 11 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 11 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 3-11\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89ac7b0b-d19b-54f6-8432-fe81b25a3cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351880} {:answer/id #uuid "5c24d547-fa21-5734-926a-75d7c4e43c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351881} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4357cd-5209-5837-a62b-373c2af25a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351882} {:answer/id #uuid "a68bab39-dee9-5834-a86b-1aed6bd4bfde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351883} {:answer/id #uuid "74b58b99-5ca2-50e3-942e-e019eea2bc64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a68bab39-dee9-5834-a86b-1aed6bd4bfde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351883} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e000246-b63c-5325-9cc7-87e3db3d6cba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4768990c-24b9-53c9-b1cb-20145c1acc12" :question/math-academy-id "q-82716" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 19 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 22 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}22 & 19 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & 19 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
